A comment is a note in the margin, attached to a phrase, a sentence or a whole paragraph. It
stays with the words while you keep writing.

## Add a comment

From the text you are writing:

- Select the words to comment on and press `Ctrl+Alt+M`.
- Put the cursor in a paragraph and press `Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M` to comment on the whole paragraph.
- Right-click in the text for *Comment on this paragraph*. *Add comment* joins it as soon as you
  have selected something.

The same two commands are in the *Format* menu.

A new card opens in the margin, with an empty box and the cursor already in it.

## Sign your comments

If no name is set for you on this computer, a message says your comments are unsigned. Its *Set
your name* button opens *Settings ▸ User*.

## Reply, resolve, delete

Every card in the margin carries a chevron button. Behind it:

- *Reply* adds a turn to the thread.
- *Resolve* marks the point settled. The card leaves the margin and the comment stays in the
  panels.
- *Delete* removes a comment, *Delete reply* a single reply, and *Delete all comments here* a
  whole batch at once. A short message follows, with an *Undo* button.

Right-click an entry in either panel to act on it there. That is where you reopen a resolved
comment, since its card has left the margin.

Every comment and every reply is edited in place. Bold and italic are in the right-click menu of
the box you are typing in.

## Where comments appear

The same threads show up in more than one place:

- In the margin beside the text you are writing. A commented phrase is tinted, a whole-paragraph
  comment gets a bracket, and a dotted line joins each mark to its card.
- In the *Comments* panel on the left of the window, listing the comments in the project.
- In the *This document* panel on the right, listing the comments on the item you are editing.

Clicking a comment in either panel opens its item. If the comment still has its text, the cursor
lands on the words it is about. *Tools ▸ Comments* hides or shows the marks and the margin.

## Filter and sort

The two panels share one set of filters: *All*, *Open*, *Resolved* and *Orphaned*. Change the
filter in one and the other follows.

Next to them, one button cycles the order, shared the same way: *In document order*, *Newest
first*, *Grouped by reader*.

## Notes from several readers

*Grouped by reader* is for a manuscript back from several people. Grouping puts each reader's
notes together, each reader's own still in document order, with the name on the left.

In either panel, a comment also says how many readers are on the same passage ("3 readers") when
more than one is.

## When a comment loses its text

A comment remembers the words it was attached to, not a position in the file.

If those words are deleted or rewritten past recognition, the comment is marked *Lost its text*
rather than attached to the wrong sentence. The *Orphaned* filter finds them.

## Sending comments out and back

Word and LibreOffice files carry your comments out and bring your reader's back. An export style
has *Include comments* and *Include round-trip markers* for that. See
[the round trip](:help-round-trip) for sending a manuscript out and reading what comes home.
